  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable to slightly above average temperatures \nand dry conditions through Saturday.\n\nGOES water vapor channels continue to show a split flow pattern\nearly this morning. A northern stream trough is across the \nGreat Lakes, and there is an associated cold front pushing \nacross the Eastern Great Lakes. Meanwhile, the southern stream \nis evident in the cloud pattern to the N and NE of hurricane \nIsaias, with a trough in vicinity of TX. Mostly clear and not as\ncool locally ahead of the cold front, with temperatures mainly \nin the 50s to lower 60s, although some decoupling has occurred \nover interior S-central VA and interior NE NC where temperatures\nhave dropped in the upper 40s.\n\nThe cold front slides across the region this afternoon. Mixing \nassociated with the front and downslope flow should allow for \nhigh temperatures to rise above average and into the upper 70s \nto mid 80s. Mostly clear tonight behind to front with low \ntemperatures ranging from the mid/upper 40s NW to the upper \n50s/lower 60s SE. Temperatures trend down to near seasonal \naverages Friday and Saturday. Not necessarily impactful, but \nstatistical guidance is on the order of 4-8F cooler than NBM \nguidance for lows late Friday night/early Saturday morning \n(lower 50s to lower 60s NBM vs. mid/upper 40s to upper 50s MOS).\nDry conditions continue through Saturday. However, a gradual \nincrease in high levels clouds is expected as upper level \nmoisture spreads over the region well in advance of Isaias.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture and rain chances return Saturday night\ninto early Monday.\n\nBy Sunday and Monday, there is increasing confidence that \nmoisture gets pulled northward and then pushed eastward across \nthe region as Isaias makes landfall and merges with an upper \ntrough in vicinity of the Mid-South. Confidence in timing is \nmoderate as there is still some temporal differences in the \nmodel guidance. Of the 00z/08 deterministic global models and \nensemble means, the EC AIFS remains to most progressive showing \naccumulating rainfall as early as the 18z Sat to 00z Sun time \nperiod. The bulk of the guidance (ECMWF, EPS, EC AIFS ens, CMC, \nand GEPS) are generally in the 06z-12z Sun timeframe for the \nonset of accumulating rainfall, and the GFS/GEFS are slightly \nslower and generally after 12z Sun. Incoming moisture will \ninitially need to contend with a 1030mb high, which is progged \nto still be in vicinity of Cape Cod by 00z Sun, so a somewhat \nslower onset is logical. However, once deeper moisture arrives \nrain should begin quickly as 00z ensemble guidance continues to \nshow PW values at or above 200% of normal along with a plume of \ninstability surging N ahead of the trough. There is still spread\nbetween the 10th and 90th percentile QPF in the 00z/08 ensemble\nguidance. In general, the 10th percentile (reasonable low-end) \naccumulated precip through 00z Tuesday is 0.25-0.75\", while the \n90th percentile is on the order of 1.5-3.0\". The 50th percentile\n(most likely case) shows a generalized 0.75-1.5\" of rainfall, \nwith the highest rainfall totals in the southern half of the \narea as there is a hint in the guidance of more enhanced \nrainfall along a coastal trough. The presence of a coastal \ntrough would also suggest that an in situ wedge develops \nresulting in cooler temperatures and more stable conditions \nSunday across the Piedmont. Deeper moisture erodes Sunday night \ninto Monday. However, there are lingering PoPs as the upper \ntrough swings across the region. Behind this system, drier air \nwill filter back in later Monday through the middle of the week.\nTemperatures should remain near average late this weekend \nthrough the middle of next week.",
